Setup API Gateway for API Management
Steps to be Performed:

1- Create a REST API in API Gateway
2- Integrate Lambda with API Gateway using Lambda Proxy Integration
3- Enable CORS
4- Deploy the API
5- Integrate API Gateway with React Application and Test

#1 Create a REST API in API Gateway:

Navigate to the API Gateway Console
Create a New REST API:
Click on Create API.
Choose REST API.
Click Build under REST API.

Configure the API:
Enter a name for your API (e.g., CloudDictionaryAPIGateway).
Choose Create API.


#2 Integrate Lambda with API Gateway using Lambda Proxy Integration:

Add an Integration:
After the API is created, click on Create Resource.
Name the resource as /get-definition, which will handle fetching the definition of terms.
Under Resource Path, enter get-definition.

Make sure that the resource name is /get-definition only, since the frontend code is configured to handle the following resource name.
Create a Method for the Resource:
Select the /get-definition resource.
Click on Create Method.

Select GET from the dropdown.
In the Integration Type dropdown, select Lambda Function.
Check the option for Use Lambda Proxy integration. This allows API Gateway to forward the entire request to the Lambda function, including headers, query parameters, and body.
Choose the region where your Lambda function is located.
In the Lambda Function field, search for and select your Lambda function (e.g., FetchTermFromDynamoDB).
Click on Create Method.

#3 Enable CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing):

To ensure that your React front-end can make requests to the API, you need to enable CORS for this API.
Enable CORS:
Go to the /get-definition resource and select Enable CORS.
Check the options Gateway responses and Access-Control-Allow-Methods as given below. Click on Save. 

#4 Deploy the API:

Click on Deploy API.
In the stage dropdown, click on New Stage and enter the stage name (eg: dev) Click on Deploy. 
Obtain the API URL - After deployment, you’ll see a Invoke URL (e.g., https://htbc5ljxl6.execute-api.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/dev).

#5 Integrate API Gateway with Your React Application and Test:

Now that your API is set up, you'll need to connect it with your React front-end. This will allow users to search for terms and get their definitions from the API.
Update the API URL in App.js:
Open your React application and navigate to the App.js file.
In the apiUrl variable, replace the placeholder URL with your API Gateway URL:
javascript
const apiUrl = '<ENTER YOUR API_URL>'; // Replace with your API Gateway URL


#6 Push Changes to GitHub:

1. Commit the Changes:

After making the changes to your App.js file, commit the updates. In the terminal, add the commands:
python
git add .
git commit -m "Integrate API Gateway with React front-end"

2. Push the Changes:

Push the changes to your GitHub repository.
python
git push origin main
This will update your GitHub repository with the changes you made to integrate the API Gateway with your React application.
Once the changes are pushed to GitHub, AWS Amplify automatically detects the changes and triggers a new deployment.
This may take a few minutes depending on the size of your application.
You can monitor the deployment status in the Amplify Console under the Deployments section.
Test the application. Type any term that was available in the DynamoDB table. For eg: AWS KMS

You can now try searching for terms.
You will only be able to search for terms that are present in your DynamoDB table or the ones you inserted from records.json.
These terms are case and space sensitive so you will need to make sure you search for the exact term.
